The forces that move markets intraday
Think of intraday price movement as the result of several forces acting simultaneously. Some are planned, some are random, some are structural.

1. Global and Macro overnight setup
This is what sets the tone before 9:15 AM. Everything that happened while Indian markets were closed.
US markets:

Dow, S&P 500, Nasdaq closing level
Whether they closed up or down by more than 0.5%
Futures movement overnight (S&P futures, Nasdaq futures)
A US market crash of 2%+ almost guarantees a gap down open in India

Asian markets (open before India):

Nikkei (Japan) — opens 5:30 AM IST
Hang Seng (Hong Kong) — opens 6:30 AM IST
Shanghai composite
If Asia is red when India opens, that pressure carries over

GIFT Nifty:

The single most important pre-open indicator
Trades from 6:30 AM IST
Absorbs all overnight US and Asian moves
A reliable 75–80% directional predictor for opening
Gap between GIFT Nifty and previous Nifty close tells you exactly how much the market wants to gap

Dollar index (DXY):

Strong dollar = weak rupee = FII outflows from India
Weak dollar = strong rupee = FII inflows
This matters especially for IT stocks and export-heavy sectors

US bond yields:

High US 10-year yield = expensive dollar = capital flows out of emerging markets like India
This is why Indian markets fell sharply when US yields rose in 2022-23

Crude oil:

India imports 85% of its oil
Brent crude above $90 = negative for India macro = Aviation, Paint, Tyre, FMCG stocks hurt
Crude spike is almost always bearish for India except for Oil & Gas stocks (ONGC, Oil India)

Gold:

When gold spikes, it signals fear in global markets
Usually negative for equities


2. Currency moves
USDINR:

Rupee weakening (say from 83 to 84) is negative for import-heavy sectors
Positive for IT exporters (TCS, Infosys, Wipro earn in dollars)
FIIs see rupee depreciation as a loss on their India investments — they sell
A sharp rupee move during market hours creates sector rotation within the session

USDINR is live during market hours — it moves tick by tick and influences big institutional decisions in real time.

3. India VIX
The fear gauge. The single number that tells you the expected daily range.

VIX below 12 — complacent market, low volatility, option sellers win
VIX 12–18 — normal market
VIX 18–25 — elevated fear, trending moves, option buyers win
VIX above 25 — panic, erratic moves, anything can happen

Intraday VIX movement matters:

If VIX spikes suddenly mid-session — something scared the market (news, geopolitical event)
If VIX collapses — market is calming, likely to consolidate
VIX × Nifty / 1000 gives you the expected daily range — this sets your SL and target universe for the day


4. Opening range and first 15 minutes
The 9:15–9:30 window is not just the start — it's a diagnostic.
What gets established:

Opening gap (up or down vs previous close)
Opening range high and low
Whether gap fills immediately or holds
Volume in the first 5 minutes — high volume confirms the direction

Gap behavior patterns:

Gap up + holds above gap midpoint at 9:30 = gap and go (bullish)
Gap up + immediately fills = fade the gap (bearish)
Gap down + holds below gap midpoint = gap and continue (bearish)
Gap down + immediately fills = reversal opportunity (bullish)

The first 15 minutes sets the session bias for most traders. Most ORB (Opening Range Breakout) strategies are built on this.

5. Institutional flow — FII and DII
FII (Foreign Institutional Investors):

They move markets. Full stop.
FII buying = sustained upward pressure, especially on large caps and index stocks
FII selling = sustained downward pressure
You can see FII cash market data at 3:30 PM EOD but intraday clues come from:

Index futures open interest building
Nifty Bank Nifty sustained directional moves
Currency (if rupee is strengthening, FII money is likely coming in)



DII (Domestic Institutional Investors — mutual funds, insurance):

Often act as counterparty to FIIs
When FIIs sell, DIIs often buy (SIP money keeps flowing)
DII buying provides a floor during FII sell-offs
Heavy DII buying mid-session can reverse a downtrend

Participant-wise OI data (EOD):

Shows what FII, DII, Pro desks, retail did in derivatives
FII adding index put longs = they expect market to fall
FII adding index call longs = they expect market to rise
This is why your system needs participant-wise OI as a data source


6. Nifty and Bank Nifty options market
This is the most underappreciated intraday force for Indian markets specifically.
Max pain:

The strike price where maximum options expire worthless
On expiry day (Tuesday for Nifty, Thursday for Bank Nifty), price gravitates toward max pain
This is not a coincidence — market makers and option writers actively push price there
Your system tracking this and alerting near max pain on expiry is genuine edge

PCR (Put Call Ratio):

PCR below 0.7 = too many calls = market likely to fall (contrarian)
PCR above 1.3 = too many puts = market likely to rise (contrarian)
PCR crossing key levels mid-session signals change in sentiment

Gamma and GEX (Gamma Exposure):

Positive GEX = market makers are long gamma = they buy dips and sell rallies = mean-reverting market
Negative GEX = market makers are short gamma = they chase moves = trending market
This explains why some days Nifty oscillates in a 50-point range and other days trends 300 points in one direction

OI walls:

If 24,000 CE has 1 crore+ open interest, that's a resistance wall — market struggles to go above
If 23,500 PE has 1 crore+ OI, that's a support floor
When these walls break (OI unwinding at a strike), it's like a dam breaking — accelerated move
Your system tracking OI at key strikes every 3 minutes captures this in real time

Straddle unwinding on expiry:

As expiry approaches and market stays near ATM, straddles lose value fast
This can create unusual intraday calm followed by a sudden spike


7. Sector rotation
Markets don't move uniformly. Different sectors lead and lag throughout the day.
Morning sector leadership sets the tone:

If IT is leading at open — market is defensive, risk-off globally
If Banks are leading — domestic liquidity good, FII buying
If metals are leading — global commodity cycle positive
If FMCG is leading — defensive posturing, market may not sustain rally

Sector rotation during the session:

9:15–11:00 — leaders establish
11:00–13:30 — rotation begins, money moves from hot sectors to lagging
13:30–15:00 — second move, institutional repositioning
15:00–15:30 — closing auction pressure, window dressing

RRG (Relative Rotation Graph) applied intraday:

Tracking which sectors are gaining relative strength during the session
A sector moving from lagging to improving = early rotation signal


8. Corporate events and announcements
This is the scheduled intraday catalyst category.
Results season:

Quarterly results announced during market hours cause immediate stock moves
Beat = 3–10% up in minutes
Miss = 3–15% down in minutes
Guidance matters more than the actual number sometimes

Board meetings:

Dividend declaration = immediate gap up (2–5%)
Buyback announcement = immediate spike (5–15%)
Merger or acquisition = can be 10–30% move
Rights issue = usually negative (dilution)
QIP announcement = negative short term (dilution)

Credit rating changes during market hours:

As we discussed — immediate price impact
Downgrade mid-session = panic selling within 30 minutes

Order wins:

Large infrastructure, defense, IT services companies
A ₹1000 Cr+ order win announcement mid-session = 2–8% spike
First move is fast — within 15–30 minutes of NSE filing

Management changes:

CEO/CFO resignation during market hours = immediate sell-off
Well-known CEO joining a company = positive spike


9. Technical levels and price memory
Markets have memory. Certain price levels act as magnets and barriers.
Key levels that matter intraday:

Previous day high (PDH) and previous day low (PDL) — most important for intraday
Previous week high and low
Round numbers — 50, 100, 500, 1000 — psychological magnets
52-week highs and lows — breakout or breakdown levels
VWAP — the reference point for institutional traders
Opening range high and low
Session S/R from prior consolidation zones

How they create intraday moves:

Price approaches PDH → resistance → pullback → if breaks with volume → acceleration
Price falls to VWAP → institutional buyers step in → bounce
Price breaks below round number with volume → stop losses trigger → cascade down


10. Delivery vs intraday volume ratio
Unique to India — NSE publishes this daily.
High delivery % + price move up = conviction buying (institutions accumulating)
Low delivery % + price spike = traders chasing (weak hands, likely to reverse)
High delivery % + price falling = distribution (institutions exiting quietly)
Intraday you can approximate this by watching:

Volume spikes with price direction
Whether volume is sustained or one-time
First 30 minutes delivery vs subsequent hours


11. News flow — real time
Scheduled news:

RBI monetary policy (6-8 times/year) — massive intraday moves
Union budget (once/year) — biggest single day move event
Economic Survey
CPI and WPI inflation data
GST collections monthly
IIP (industrial production) data

Unscheduled news:

Geopolitical events (India-Pakistan tension = defense stocks spike, market falls)
Natural disasters
Regulatory actions by SEBI, RBI, IRDA
Supreme Court orders on companies
Government policy changes (import duties, export bans)
Global events — US Fed surprise statements, China data

The problem with news:

By the time you read it, the algo has already acted
Your edge is not being first to the news
Your edge is knowing which stocks are set up technically to move on the news


12. Algo and HFT behavior
Algorithms control 50–60% of NSE volume. They create patterns you need to understand.
VWAP algorithms:

Large institutions use VWAP algos to execute big orders
They buy gradually throughout the day to minimize impact
This creates steady directional pressure that looks like organic buying
If you see a stock slowly creeping up all day with consistent volume — it's likely VWAP execution

Momentum algorithms:

These chase price — they buy when price breaks above a level
They amplify breakouts
When a breakout fails, these algos also reverse fast — sharp whipsaws

Mean reversion algorithms:

They fade extreme moves
They buy when RSI is very oversold, sell when very overbought
They create the 11 AM–1 PM choppy period where everything bounces around

Stop hunting:

Algos know where retail stops are (just below round numbers, just below obvious support)
You'll see price dip exactly to those levels, trigger stops, then reverse
This is the liquidity grab pattern your system is designed to detect


13. Time of day effects — India specific
Markets behave differently at different times. This is structural, not random.
9:15–9:30 — Opening chaos:

High volatility, wide spreads
Gap fills or extensions happen here
Avoid trading unless you have a pre-planned setup

9:30–11:00 — First trend window:

Best time for momentum trades
ORB setups, gap and go
Institutions establish positions

11:00–11:30 — First exhaustion:

Morning trend often stalls here
Pullbacks happen
Watch for reversal or continuation with volume confirmation

11:30–13:30 — Lunch zone:

Volume thins
Fake breakouts common
Mean reversion strategies work better here
Many experienced traders reduce position size or sit out

13:30–14:30 — European open influence:

European markets open around 1:30 PM IST
European buying/selling in Indian ADRs and related stocks
Can create a second directional move

14:30–15:00 — Second trend window:

Institutional repositioning before close
Real moves often happen here
Good time for momentum trades with the trend

15:00–15:20 — Closing pressure:

Index rebalancing, ETF buying/selling
Window dressing by mutual funds (month end, quarter end especially)
Positions being squared off (MIS auto-square starts 3:20 PM)
Sharp moves possible but dangerous to trade

15:20–15:30 — Closing auction:

Don't trade this unless you know exactly what you're doing


14. Expiry effects — unique to India
India has weekly expiries which create unique intraday patterns.
Tuesday (Nifty weekly expiry):

Max pain effect strongest in last 2 hours
Gamma is extremely high near ATM
Small Nifty moves cause huge P&L swings for option holders
Volatility often collapses during the day as time value bleeds

Thursday (Bank Nifty weekly expiry):

Same dynamics but Bank Nifty is more volatile
Often sees a sharp move in one direction in last 1 hour
Called "expiry volatility" — can be 100–200 points in minutes

Monthly expiry (last Thursday):

Biggest OI moves
Max pain effect is strongest
FII positioning shows most clearly
Often the biggest single intraday move of the month


15. Operator activity in mid and small caps
This is the dark force that nobody talks about officially but every experienced trader knows about.
What operators do:

Accumulate quietly over days/weeks
Release positive news or create buzz
Pump the stock during market hours
Distribute (sell) into the retail buying

How it shows up intraday:

Sudden volume spike 3–5× with no news
Price moves 5–10% in 30 minutes on a small cap
Upper circuits on illiquid stocks
Unusual options activity before a news event

Your system's defense:

pump_dump_score — detects this pattern
Low quality score + no fundamentals + sudden volume = flag
Blacklist catches ASM/GSM stocks which are often operator driven
Filter out stocks below a liquidity threshold entirely


How all these interact — a real example
Say it's 10:30 AM. Nifty has gapped up 0.8% at open. Gift Nifty was up 1% overnight. US markets closed strong. The following is happening:

Banks are leading (FII buying signal)
USDINR is strengthening (risk on)
India VIX is falling (complacency, call sellers winning)
PCR is at 1.1 (moderate put protection, not extreme)
OI wall at 24,000 CE is intact (resistance)
RELIANCE just announced a large order win (stock specific catalyst)

Now RELIANCE shows a long buildup signal — price up 1.4%, OI up 4.3%, volume 3× average, above VWAP, VWAP slope positive.
Every macro and micro force is aligned. Market is up, sector is leading, stock has a fresh catalyst, technical picture is clean. This is a high confidence signal — maybe 0.88–0.92.
Now imagine the same RELIANCE long buildup signal but:

Nifty is down 0.5%
VIX is rising
Banks are falling
USDINR is weakening

Same stock-level signal, completely different context. Confidence drops to maybe 0.55. Market regime is working against it.

