The fundamental insight
The message quality problem is actually a context collapse problem.
You have:

15 macro forces
20+ technical indicators
Fundamentals
Event calendar
Rating actions
Options data
Sector data
Time of day
Expiry effects

A human cannot process all of this. So the system must collapse all of it into a verdict with evidence.
Not a data dump. A verdict.
The difference between a bad alert and a great alert is whether it answers three questions instantly:

What is happening right now — the trigger
Why this matters — the context
What to do — the actionable part


The architecture of a great alert message
Think of it like a newspaper article. The inverted pyramid structure:
HEADLINE        — what happened, in 5 words
LEAD            — why it matters, in 2 lines  
SUPPORTING FACTS — top 3-4 confirming factors
RISK CONTEXT    — what could go wrong
ACTION PARAMS   — entry zone, SL, target
CONFIDENCE      — single number, no ambiguity
Every word in the message must earn its place. If removing it loses nothing, remove it.

The scoring engine — how confidence is actually computed
This is the heart of the system. The confidence score is not a number you pick — it is the output of a multi-layer evaluation.
Think of it as a funnel with gates and boosters.

Layer 0 — Hard gates (binary kill switches)
These are not scores. They are hard stops. If any of these fail, the signal is killed. Period. No message sent.
Is stock in blacklist?           → KILL
Is stock in F&O ban?             → KILL
Is stock newly listed (<30 days)?→ KILL
Is promoter pledge > 50%?        → KILL
Is it a long signal on a loss making company? → KILL
Is delivery quality score < 20?  → KILL
Is liquidity too low (<5 Cr avg daily turnover)? → KILL
Is it within 09:15–09:30 window? → KILL
Is it lunch zone (11:30–13:30) and not a strong setup? → KILL
If any gate triggers — silent discard. No message, no log to user. These are not borderline cases.

Layer 1 — Market regime score (−30 to +30)
Before looking at the stock, look at the market. Is the environment friendly or hostile to this signal type?
Market direction:
Nifty trending up strongly today    → +10
Nifty flat / sideways               →   0
Nifty trending down strongly        → −15
VIX state:
VIX falling + below 15              → +10 (calm, momentum works)
VIX flat                            →   0
VIX rising + above 20               → −10 (fear, mean reversion works better)
VIX above 25                        → −20 (panic, only high-conviction plays)
Global setup:
GIFT Nifty gap aligned with signal  → +5
GIFT Nifty neutral                  →  0
GIFT Nifty against signal           → −10
FII flow:
FII net buyers today (cash)         → +5
FII net sellers today               → −5
Time of day:
9:30–11:00 (prime window)           → +5
13:30–15:00 (second window)         → +3
11:30–13:30 (lunch zone)            → −10
15:00+ (closing pressure)           → −15
Expiry effect:
Expiry day, signal aligned with max pain direction → +5
Expiry day, signal against max pain               → −10
Non-expiry day                                    →  0
Regime score range: −30 to +30
If regime score is below −15, kill the signal regardless of stock quality.

Layer 2 — Sector score (−15 to +15)
Is the stock's sector helping or hurting?
Sector is top 3 performing today    → +10
Sector is average                   →   0
Sector is bottom 3 performing today → −10
Sector RS rank improving intraday   → +5
Sector RS rank deteriorating        → −5
This matters because a breakout in a leading sector has institutional backing. A breakout in a lagging sector is fighting the tide.

Layer 3 — Stock fundamental score (−20 to +20)
Long-term quality of the business. This doesn't change minute to minute.
Quality score > 70                  → +10
Quality score 50–70                 → +5
Quality score 30–50                 →  0
Quality score < 30 (long signal)    → −20

Debt/Equity < 0.5                   → +5
Debt/Equity > 2                     → −10

Promoter holding > 50%              → +3
Promoter pledge > 25%               → −10

YoY revenue growing > 15%          → +5
YoY revenue shrinking               → −10

Recent credit rating upgrade        → +5
Recent credit rating downgrade      → −15

Layer 4 — Technical alignment score (−20 to +30)
Is the stock technically set up for this signal?
Trend:
Daily trend: strong uptrend (for long signal)  → +10
Daily trend: uptrend                           →  +5
Daily trend: sideways                          →   0
Daily trend: downtrend (for long signal)       → −15
5-min momentum:
RSI 5m: 50–65 (healthy, room to run)  → +10
RSI 5m: 40–50 (recovering)            →  +5
RSI 5m: 65–75 (strong but watch)      →  +3
RSI 5m: > 75 (overbought, be careful) → −10
RSI 5m: > 80 (extreme, avoid long)    → −20
VWAP:
Price above VWAP + VWAP slope positive → +10
Price above VWAP, flat slope            →  +5
Price below VWAP (for long signal)      → −10
Volatility setup:
BB compression (squeeze)        → +5 (energy building)
BB expansion (breakout confirmed) → +3
Normal                          →  0
Volume:
Volume 3×+ above 20d avg        → +5
Volume 1.5–3× above avg         → +3
Volume below avg                → −5 (weak signal)
Key level:
Breakout above PDH with volume  → +5
Near 52-week high               → +3
Far from any key level          →  0
At strong resistance            → −5

Layer 5 — Signal-specific score (0 to 20)
The quality of the trigger itself.
For OI buildup signals:
OI change > 10%                 → +10
OI change 5–10%                 →  +5
OI change 3–5%                  →  +3
Price change > 2%               → +5
Price change 1–2%               → +3
Volume 3×+ confirming           → +5
For breakout signals:
New 52-week high                → +10
Clean break above resistance    →  +5
Volume 2×+ confirming           →  +5
For announcement signals:
Result beat YoY > 20%           → +10
Result beat YoY 10–20%          →  +5
Credit upgrade (AA or above)    →  +8
Credit downgrade to junk        → +10 (short signal)
Order win > 5× market cap / 10  →  +8

Layer 6 — Confluence bonus (0 to 20)
This is where the magic happens. When multiple independent signals align simultaneously on the same stock, confidence goes up non-linearly.
OI buildup + volume surge (same stock, same time)          → +10
Breakout + result beat (technical + fundamental catalyst)   → +15
Rating upgrade + technical breakout                         → +12
OI buildup + sector leading + VWAP above + VIX falling     → +15
Multiple brokerage upgrades in same week + technical setup  → +10
The key principle: independent confirmations multiply confidence, not add. If 3 unrelated forces all point the same direction simultaneously, that's not coincidence — that's institutional accumulation, that's real.

Final confidence computation
base_score = 0.50  (every signal starts here)

regime_contribution    = regime_score / 30 × 0.15
sector_contribution    = sector_score / 15 × 0.10
fundamental_contribution = fundamental_score / 20 × 0.15
technical_contribution = technical_score / 30 × 0.20
signal_contribution    = signal_score / 20 × 0.15
confluence_bonus       = confluence_score / 20 × 0.15

final_confidence = base_score 
                 + regime_contribution
                 + sector_contribution  
                 + fundamental_contribution
                 + technical_contribution
                 + signal_contribution
                 + confluence_bonus
Thresholds:
< 0.55  → discard silently
0.55–0.65 → low confidence, send only if confluence present
0.65–0.75 → good signal, send with normal priority
0.75–0.85 → strong signal, send with high priority
> 0.85  → exceptional signal, send with urgent tag

The message itself — anatomy of a great alert
Now that you have a confidence score and all the contributing factors, you build the message.
The message has exactly 6 sections. Nothing more.

Section 1 — The headline (1 line)
🟢 RELIANCE — Long Buildup | Confidence: HIGH (0.88)
Three things only: stock, signal type, confidence tier. The emoji encodes direction instantly (green = long bias, red = short bias, yellow = neutral/watch).
Confidence tiers:
> 0.85 → HIGH
0.75–0.85 → GOOD  
0.65–0.75 → MODERATE
0.55–0.65 → WATCHLIST (don't trade, observe)

Section 2 — The trigger (2–3 lines)
What actually happened. The raw facts that fired the signal.
Trigger: OI +4.3% | Price +1.4% | Vol 3.1× avg
Time: 10:47 AM | Price at trigger: ₹2,841
No interpretation here. Just the facts.

Section 3 — The context (3–4 lines)
Why the environment supports this signal. Only the factors that are GREEN. Don't list everything.
✓ Nifty up 0.6% | VIX falling (14.2)
✓ Energy sector leading (+1.8%)
✓ VWAP: above | RSI(5m): 61 | Trend: uptrend
✓ Quality: 74/100 | Revenue growth YoY: +18%

Section 4 — The risk flags (only if any exist)
Only include this section if something is worth flagging. Don't always include it.
⚠ RSI approaching 65 — limited upside momentum
⚠ Result expected in 4 days — hold size conservative
If no flags — skip this section entirely. Absence of warning is itself a positive signal.

Section 5 — Action parameters
Entry zone: ₹2,838 – ₹2,845 (current: ₹2,841)
Stop loss:  ₹2,810 (below VWAP + PDL, ATR-based)
Target 1:   ₹2,878 (1R — partial exit)
Target 2:   ₹2,915 (2R — trail remainder)
Max hold:   15:20 IST (mandatory flat)
This is the most important section for an actionable alert. Without this, the alert is just information. With this, it's a complete idea.
How SL is calculated:

Take the lower of: structure-based (PDL, VWAP) OR ATR-based (1.5 × ATR14 from entry)
This ensures SL is never arbitrary

How target is calculated:

Target 1 = 1R (risk × 1 = same distance as SL but upward)
Target 2 = 2R (ride with trail)
Never promise more than 2R without exceptional confluence


Section 6 — The why in plain English (1–2 sentences)
This is optional but powerful. A brief human-readable summary of why this signal is worth looking at.
Summary: Reliance showing institutional accumulation — OI building as price rises
with sector tailwind and healthy fundamentals. Clean setup above VWAP.
This makes the alert feel like it came from an analyst, not a machine. It builds trust in the system over time because you can verify it yourself.

