First — understanding the phenomenon properly
The phrases get mixed up. Let me clarify exactly what each means.
"Buy the rumor, sell the news"

Anticipation of a positive event causes price to rise BEFORE the event
When the event actually happens, the buying is exhausted
Traders who bought the rumor now sell into the news
Result: stock falls ON the day of good news
Classic example: Nifty rallies 3% in the week before RBI rate cut, then falls 1% the day RBI actually cuts

"Buy the news, sell the rumor" (less common phrase, same phenomenon reversed)

Sometimes used when negative rumors cause selling
The actual news turns out less bad than feared
Relief rally happens ON the news day
Example: Stock falls 8% on merger rumors over 2 weeks, actual merger announced at fair price, stock rallies

The underlying force is the same in both cases:
Price moves to price in an EXPECTED event. When the event arrives, the expectation is resolved. Resolution removes the uncertainty premium. Price adjusts back.

The four scenarios that matter
Before anything else, recognize which scenario you are in.

Scenario 1 — Anticipated positive event, good outcome
Example: Result expected to be strong, result actually strong.
What happens:

Stock already up 5–15% in days before result
Result comes in — beat is confirmed
Initial spike of 2–3% at announcement
Then selling begins as early buyers exit
Stock often ends below the spike high by end of day
But 2–5 days later, a second leg up as fresh buyers come in

How to handle:

Don't chase the initial spike
Wait for the first pullback after the spike
Enter on the pullback if volume declines on the dip
The real move is often the second wave, not the first


Scenario 2 — Anticipated positive event, disappointing outcome
Example: Result expected strong, result misses.
What happens:

Stock already up 5–15% pre-result
Result disappoints — miss confirmed
Stock crashes — not just the miss, but the UNWINDING of the anticipation premium
Move is double — reversal of pre-run + fresh selling on bad news
This is brutal. 10–20% down in a day is possible

How to handle:

This is the most dangerous scenario for longs
If a stock has run 10%+ before its result — size down or avoid longs entirely
The system should flag stocks that have run significantly pre-event as HIGH RISK
After the miss, the short signal is very high confidence


Scenario 3 — Anticipated negative event, bad outcome
Example: Downgrade expected, downgrade happens.
What happens:

Stock already down 5–15% on the fear
Actual downgrade confirmed
Further selling but usually less severe than the pre-event fall
Often a dead cat bounce after the initial shock
Real bottom only after 2–3 days when forced sellers finish

How to handle:

The first reaction is often overdone
Look for the bounce setup 1–2 days after
Don't short into already beaten down stocks — bad risk/reward
The bounce is the trade, not the continuation of the fall


Scenario 4 — Anticipated negative event, better than feared outcome
Example: Result feared to be bad, actual result is okay.
What happens:

Stock already down 10–20% pre-result on fear
Result comes in — not as bad as feared
Relief rally — sometimes violent — 5–15% in one session
This is the "sell the rumor, buy the news" scenario
These are some of the best asymmetric trades

How to handle:

This is a high-reward setup if you catch it
Look for stocks that have been beaten down significantly pre-event
Check: is the fear overdone relative to actual business fundamentals?
If quality score is reasonable but stock has fallen 20%+ on rumors — watch for the reversal signal at the event


How to systematically detect which scenario you are in
This is the design question. How does the system know?
Signal 1 — Pre-event price run measurement
The system measures how much a stock has already moved BEFORE the event.
pre_event_run_pct = (current_price - price_10d_ago) / price_10d_ago × 100
Classification:
pre_event_run > +15%  → BUY_RUMOR_IN_PLAY (long exhaustion risk)
pre_event_run +5% to +15% → MILD_ANTICIPATION
pre_event_run −5% to +5% → NO_SIGNIFICANT_ANTICIPATION
pre_event_run −5% to −15% → MILD_FEAR_PRICED
pre_event_run < −15% → SELL_RUMOR_IN_PLAY (short exhaustion, reversal risk)
This single number tells you how much of the event is already priced in.

Signal 2 — Options implied volatility behavior
Options traders know about upcoming events. They buy protection. This shows up in IV.
Pre-event IV expansion:

IV rising steadily as event approaches = market pricing uncertainty = event not yet priced
Means the move hasn't happened yet

IV crush:

When the event happens, uncertainty resolves
IV collapses — sometimes 30–50% in one session
This IV crush amplifies price moves in the direction of the news

What the system tracks:
iv_vs_5d_avg = current_atm_iv / 5d_avg_atm_iv

iv_vs_5d_avg > 1.5  → IV elevated, event approaching, uncertainty premium high
iv_vs_5d_avg > 2.0  → IV very elevated, event imminent
iv_vs_5d_avg < 0.7  → IV crushed, event just passed
When IV is very elevated AND pre-event run is >15% → classic buy-the-rumor setup in play.

Signal 3 — Volume pattern before the event
Smart money moves first. They know before anyone else (legally or otherwise).
Accumulation pattern before positive event:

Delivery volume rising over 5–10 days
Price creeping up on above average volume
OBV (On Balance Volume) rising
This is buy-the-rumor accumulation in progress

Distribution pattern before negative event:

Delivery volume declining
Price falling on above average volume
OBV declining
Insiders selling before bad news

The system tracks this as:
pre_event_volume_trend = avg_volume_5d / avg_volume_20d

> 1.5  → unusual pre-event activity (information asymmetry likely)
> 2.0  → strong pre-event positioning (high buy-rumor risk)

Signal 4 — News and rumor detection
The system needs to know when something is a "known event" vs a "surprise event."
Known events (scheduled, everyone knows):

Quarterly results (board meeting date announced)
RBI policy meetings (scheduled 6 times/year)
Union budget
Dividend record dates
Annual General Meetings

For these — pre-event run is almost always present. Buy-rumor risk is HIGH.
Semi-known events (rumored but not confirmed):

Acquisition rumors
Promoter stake sale rumors
Rating action rumors
Management change rumors

For these — rumor may have circulated for days/weeks before announcement. Check pre-event run.
Surprise events (no prior knowledge):

Sudden regulatory action
Geopolitical event
Unexpected management resignation
Fraud discovery

For these — no pre-event run, first mover advantage is maximum, buy-rumor dynamic doesn't apply.

Signal 5 — The announcement arrival pattern
When the actual news hits, what the stock does in the FIRST 5 MINUTES tells you everything.
Pattern A — Spike and hold:

Stock jumps 3% on news
Stays above 2.5% for 5 minutes
Volume is 5×+ average
This is a genuine first-reaction move — more likely to continue

Pattern B — Spike and fade:

Stock jumps 3% on news
Falls back to 1.5% within 5 minutes
Volume high initially but dropping
This is buy-rumor sell-news in action — the sellers are present

Pattern C — No reaction:

News hits, stock barely moves
Fully priced in already
Neither buy nor sell signal

Pattern D — Reverse reaction:

Good news, stock falls
Bad news, stock rises
Classic buy-rumor sell-news or sell-rumor buy-news
These are COUNTER-INTUITIVE moves — very important to flag

Pattern D is the most important one to detect and signal. A stock falling on good news is telling you something. The system must catch this.

The psychological cycle — full map
Understanding the full cycle helps predict where in the cycle a stock is.
RUMOR STARTS
     ↓
First movers buy quietly
(volume rises, price creeps)
     ↓
Rumor spreads to more traders
(volume spikes, price accelerates)
     ↓
Maximum anticipation — stock at peak
(everyone who will buy has bought)
     ↓
NEWS ARRIVES
     ↓
Initial spike (late buyers jump in)
     ↓
Smart money SELLS into the spike
     ↓
Price starts falling despite good news
     ↓
Retail confused — "but the news was good?"
     ↓
Price falls back to pre-rumor levels
     ↓
MAXIMUM PESSIMISM
     ↓
Real long-term buyers enter
     ↓
Second leg up begins (slower, more sustainable)
The system needs to identify WHERE in this cycle the stock is at any given moment.

