Other psychological factors to systematize
While we're here — other psychological forces that create repeatable patterns.

Round number magnetism
Nifty at 24,000. Reliance at ₹3,000. These are not just technical levels. They are psychological anchors.
The system tracks:

Distance from nearest round number (50, 100, 500, 1000)
Volume behavior AS price approaches round numbers
Whether the stock has repeatedly failed at a round number (psychological resistance)

round_number_distance_pct < 0.5%  → AT round number (max psychological significance)
round_number_distance_pct < 2%    → APPROACHING (watch for behavior)
previous_failures_at_level > 2    → STRONG psychological resistance

Loss aversion and stop loss clustering
Retail traders cluster stops at obvious levels. Operators know this.
Common stop clustering zones:

Just below round numbers (stops at 999, 4999, 19999)
Just below previous day low
Just below a well-known moving average (200 DMA especially)

The system detects when price approaches these zones with high volume — it's either a genuine breakdown or a stop hunt.
The stop hunt signature:
Price dips below obvious support level
Volume spikes on the dip
Price recovers back above the level within 1–2 candles
Volume drops on the recovery
When this pattern appears — it's a liquidity grab. The smart trade is in the direction of the recovery, not the dip.

FOMO — Fear of Missing Out
FOMO creates the final blow-off top of a trend.
FOMO signatures:

Stock up 15–20% in 3–5 days
Volume increasing each day (more people piling in)
Social media buzz (hard to measure but correlated with volume)
RSI above 80
Price far above VWAP
New 52-week high every day

This is NOT the time to buy. This is the time the smart money is selling to the FOMO buyers.
System detects:
consecutive_up_days > 5 AND
volume_trend_5d = rising AND
rsi_5m > 78 AND
pct_from_vwap > 3%
→ FOMO_EXHAUSTION_WARNING

Capitulation — the opposite of FOMO
The mirror of FOMO. Maximum pain, everyone selling, nobody wants to hold.
Capitulation signatures:

Stock down 8–15% in 3–5 days
Volume increasing each day on the down days (panic selling)
RSI below 25
Price far below VWAP
New 52-week lows
Delivery percentage high (long-term holders finally giving up)

This is NOT the time to short. It's often the best buying opportunity.
System detects:
consecutive_down_days > 4 AND
volume_trend_5d = rising (panic) AND
rsi_5m < 22 AND
pct_below_vwap > 3% AND
delivery_pct > 60%
→ CAPITULATION_WATCH (reversal setup forming)

Anchoring bias
Traders anchor to specific prices and make irrational decisions around them.
Common anchors:

IPO price (people who bought at IPO hold on no matter what)
All-time high (people who missed the top wait for it to come back)
Round numbers (₹100, ₹500, ₹1000)
52-week high and low

How this creates tradable patterns:

Stock that crashed from ₹500 to ₹250 has massive overhead resistance at ₹350, ₹400, ₹450 — people who are "stuck" sell to break even
Stock approaching its IPO price from below often gets a relief bounce (bagholders finally at breakeven)
Stock at all-time high has no overhead resistance — breakout is clean

distance_from_ath_pct = (ath - current) / ath × 100

distance < 2%   → NEAR ALL-TIME HIGH (if breaks, clean runway)
distance = 0%   → NEW ALL-TIME HIGH (momentum signal, no resistance)
distance > 30%  → DEEP VALUE or FALLING KNIFE (check which)

Disposition effect — holding losers, selling winners
Retail traders systematically:

Sell winning positions too early (to lock in profits)
Hold losing positions too long (to avoid admitting a loss)

This creates predictable patterns:
Supply overhang at previous highs:

People who bought near a previous high and held through a crash are waiting to sell at breakeven
This creates resistance at those levels

Support at round loss levels:

People who bought at ₹500 won't sell at ₹350 (too painful) but might at ₹400
Clustering of buy orders at -20%, -30% from peak


The master psychological state classifier
All of these factors together let you classify the psychological state of any stock at any moment.
PSYCHOLOGICAL STATE:
─────────────────────────────────
FOMO_EUPHORIA      → Late buyers piling in, smart money selling
─────────────────────────────────
BUY_RUMOR          → Anticipation building, pre-event run active
─────────────────────────────────
NEUTRAL_TRENDING   → Normal price discovery, follow the trend
─────────────────────────────────
SELL_NEWS          → Good news arrived, exhaustion selling
─────────────────────────────────
FEAR_BUILDING      → Negative expectations forming, avoid longs
─────────────────────────────────
CAPITULATION       → Maximum pain, potential reversal zone
─────────────────────────────────
RELIEF_BOUNCE      → Bad news less bad than feared, recovery
─────────────────────────────────
DEAD_CAT_BOUNCE    → Temporary relief in downtrend, avoid longs
─────────────────────────────────
Each state has a different trading approach. The system identifies the state and the alert message tells you which state you're in.

How this feeds into confidence scoring
Add a new layer to the confidence computation:
Psychological alignment score (−20 to +20):
Signal is LONG + state is NEUTRAL_TRENDING    → +10
Signal is LONG + state is FOMO_EUPHORIA       → −20 (fighting exhaustion)
Signal is LONG + state is BUY_RUMOR active    → −10 (late to the party)
Signal is LONG + state is CAPITULATION        → +15 (contrarian long setup)
Signal is SHORT + state is SELL_NEWS          → +15 (sell-news confirmed)
Signal is SHORT + state is RELIEF_BOUNCE      → −15 (fighting the bounce)
Signal is either + state is NEUTRAL           →   0
This single addition to the scoring engine dramatically reduces false signals — because the biggest losses come from taking technically valid signals in psychologically wrong environments.

The single most important principle
All of these psychological factors reduce to one insight:
Price moves in anticipation. News resolves anticipation. Resolution creates the opposite of the expected move.
The system that understands this will:

Never chase a stock that has already run before its catalyst
Always look for the setup AFTER the initial reaction, not during it
Treat violent moves on news as setup opportunities for the subsequent move
Flag exhaustion before it becomes a painful position
Find the asymmetric trades where fear is overdone or excitement is overdone

