8. Relative Strength — Within Sector
We track sector RS but within-sector relative strength is even more powerful.
The idea:
In any bull run, some stocks in a sector lead. Others lag. The leaders keep leading. This is momentum persistence.
Calculation:
For each stock in NIFTY BANK:
  stock_rs = stock_5d_return / sector_5d_return

Rank all stocks in sector by this ratio:

Top quintile (ratio > 1.2) = sector leaders
Bottom quintile (ratio < 0.8) = sector laggards
Why this matters for signal confidence:
Long signal on sector leader:
→ Money is flowing into this stock specifically
→ Institutions have chosen this over peers
→ +10 to confidence

Long signal on sector laggard:
→ Even in a rising sector, this stock is weak
→ Something specific is wrong
→ −15 to confidence
The most powerful version:
Sector moving from lagging to leading in RRG
AND
A specific stock leading within that sector
AND
Technical breakout on that stock
→ Sector rotation + stock leadership + technical trigger
→ Triple confluence, very high confidence

9. Market Breadth — The Hidden Health Indicator
Breadth tells you if a market rally or fall is broad (sustainable) or narrow (fragile).
Key breadth indicators:
Advances vs Declines:
→ Nifty up 1% but 1200 stocks declining vs 800 advancing
→ Narrow rally, only large caps going up
→ Fragile — likely to reverse

Nifty up 1% and 1600 advancing vs 400 declining
→ Broad rally, healthy
→ Sustainable — trend is real

New 52-week highs:
→ More new highs than lows → bull market is healthy
→ More new lows despite flat index → internal weakness building

Percentage of stocks above 200 DMA:
→ > 70% → strong bull market
→ 40–70% → mixed market
→ < 40% → bear market or approaching
→ < 20% → extreme weakness, potential bottom
How this modifies signal confidence:
Long signal when breadth is strong (>70% above 200 DMA)
→ +10 to market regime score

Long signal when breadth is weak (<40% above 200 DMA)
→ −15 to market regime score
→ Even strong stocks struggle in bad breadth
The divergence signal:
Index making new highs
Breadth deteriorating (fewer stocks participating)
→ Distribution happening at the top
→ Smart money selling index heavyweights while rotating out
→ Market top forming
→ Reduce all long confidence scores by 10–15

10. Earnings Quality — Not Just the Number
When a result comes in, the headline EPS number is what everyone sees. But WHAT IS DRIVING that EPS matters enormously.
High quality earnings:
Revenue growing → real business growth
Operating profit growing faster than revenue → margin expansion
Cash flow from operations matching PAT → real profits not accounting tricks
Receivables growing slower than revenue → collecting money efficiently
Working capital improving → operational health
Low quality earnings:
PAT up but revenue flat → one-time items, write-backs, tax benefits
Other income driving profit → not from core business
Receivables growing faster than revenue → booking revenue but not collecting
Inventory building up → goods not selling
Cash flow negative despite positive PAT → accounting profits, not real
The system extracts from parsed PDFs:
earnings_quality_score:
  revenue_growth_yoy
  operating_profit_growth_vs_revenue_growth
  cfo_vs_pat_ratio (cash flow from operations / profit after tax)
  receivables_days_change
  inventory_days_change
High quality earnings beat (revenue + margins + cashflow all strong)
→ +15 to signal confidence
→ Move is likely to sustain

Low quality earnings beat (PAT up only due to other income)
→ −10 to signal confidence
→ Market will eventually see through this
→ Don't chase the initial spike

